1. Universal calibration is remarkably effective. With as little as 10% of tasks, regret is ≤ 1.1 % {\leq}1.1\% across all settings. The flat accuracy landscape means even imprecise threshold selection works. 2. Per-website calibration exceeds the global oracle. At X ≥ 15 X{\geq}15 – 20 % 20\% , per-website regret turns negative, exploiting website-specific threshold preferences. The advantage grows with X X , reaching + 2.91 % +2.91\% for WA-Lite entropy at X = 50 % X{=}50\% . 3. Practical recommendation: (a) With no data, use τ = 0.5 \tau{=}0.5 as a robust default;
(b) With 10–20% of tasks, run universal calibration ( ≤ 1.1 % {\leq}1.1\% regret);
(c) With 20%+ tasks and website labels, use per-website calibration to exceed the oracle.
